(module crux-status-unclamped)
;; THE SILENT SUCCESS. `status-of` turns the console loop's exit code into the
;; process status this gate hands to CI. This body returns the code unclamped.
;;
;; POSIX truncates an exit status to the low 8 bits. So a code of 256 exits 0,
;; and CI reads a PASS over a tree the gate rejected. The defect is invisible in
;; every log: there is no error line, because the gate believes it succeeded.
;;
;; This is the mutation tools/version-gate/adjudicate.llmll names in its own
;; header, and it is the direction that header calls unsafe. The shipped clamp
;; fails the other way, sending 256 to 255 rather than to 0.
;;
;; THE RANGE CLAUSE IS WHAT CATCHES THIS ONE, and that is worth stating because
;; the sibling clauses catch different mutants: a body that clamped in the wrong
;; direction would satisfy the range and be caught by the implications instead.
;; Writing both is what makes the contract discriminate rather than merely pass.
;;
;; MEASURED at v0.16.2: REFUTED. The unmutated sibling reports
;; `body-faithful: status-of` and verifies SAFE.
(def status-of [c: int] -> int
  (post (and (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
             (and (=> (< c 0) (= result 1))
                  (and (=> (> c 255) (= result 255))
                       (=> (and (>= c 0) (<= c 255)) (= result c))))))
  c)
